.nh
.TH "PB-TUNNEL-POLICY-ALLOW" "1" "Sep 2026" "Paperboat" "Paperboat CLI"

.SH NAME
pb-tunnel-policy-allow - Allow on-demand access to an exact machine port


.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBpb tunnel policy allow <machine> <port|url> [flags]\fP


.SH DESCRIPTION
Authorize on-demand access to one exact port of a machine: a bare port number or a loopback
http, https or h2c URL. --access chooses who may use it, private (the default) or team. The
policy lasts --expires (default 8h, must be positive). To change an existing policy pass its
--id together with the --generation you last saw. A replacement application on the same port
inherits the access.

.PP
JSON output is supported with --json.


.SH OPTIONS
\fB--access\fP="private"
	access audience: private or team

.PP
\fB--expires\fP=8h0m0s
	policy lifetime

.PP
\fB--generation\fP=0
	expected existing policy generation

.PP
\fB-h\fP, \fB--help\fP[=false]
	help for allow

.PP
\fB--id\fP=""
	existing policy ID to update

.PP
\fB--json\fP[=false]
	print canonical JSON


.SH OPTIONS INHERITED FROM PARENT COMMANDS
\fB--config\fP=""
	path to the CLI config file

.PP
\fB--ephemeral\fP[=false]
	use the temporary preview lifecycle

.PP
\fB--no-customization\fP[=false]
	ignore local shortcuts, command defaults, and TUI preferences

.PP
\fB--server\fP=""
	paperboat-server base URL override

.PP
\fB--workspace\fP=""
	resource workspace selector: personal or a team slug


.SH EXAMPLE
.EX
  pb tunnel policy allow homelab 3000
  pb tunnel policy allow homelab https://127.0.0.1:8443 --access team --expires 1h
  pb tunnel policy allow homelab 3000 --id pol_abc --generation 3
.EE


.SH SEE ALSO
\fBpb-tunnel-policy(1)\fP
